# Migration Repair Playbook

A Claude Code skill that adapts a large suite of end-to-end Playwright tests to an in-flight UI framework migration — one test at a time — guided by a pattern catalog that accumulates knowledge across sessions instead of re-diagnosing the same failure from scratch every time it resurfaces.

## Why this exists

When part of an application's UI is being deliberately reworked from one widget library to another, existing end-to-end tests break in large numbers, and most of those failures aren't bugs in the application — they're automation that hasn't caught up yet. The two failure modes look identical from a stack trace alone: a real regression in the new code, versus an intentional interface change the test simply hasn't been taught about. Treating every failure as a fresh regression-hunt wastes enormous effort re-discovering the same handful of root causes over and over across dozens of tests. This skill exists to draw that line explicitly and to make the second, far more common case fast.

## The pattern catalog

At the center of the skill is a living table: **symptom → root cause → fix location → status**. Before diagnosing anything new, a failure gets checked against this catalog first. Entries carry one of a few statuses — fixed suite-wide in shared helper code (so most tests hitting it are likely already fine), fixed but only verified for one specific case (worth double-checking elsewhere before assuming it's covered), or "diagnose fresh each time" for symptoms that look identical on the surface but have historically turned out to have different causes.

The catalog grew from around half a dozen entries to over twenty across several sessions, each new entry added only after being found and fixed for real — never speculated in advance. A few representative categories:

- **Widget-identity changes.** A reworked component addresses itself differently than its predecessor (a single toggle-is-the-input element instead of a separate search field; an element that loses its `id` entirely and becomes addressable only by name or role). The fix is to re-derive the selector from a fresh DOM read rather than assume the old identifier just moved.
- **Event-model mismatches.** A framework-driven field only recognizes certain interaction events to know it changed. A plain fill-and-move-on that worked against the old widget can leave a new one thinking nothing happened, so an enable/submit action downstream stays stuck.
- **Timing races that look identical to real bugs.** A value appears to save, and even survives a later same-page re-render, but is gone after an actual reload — because a shared request-sequencing behavior on the underlying form quietly drops one in-flight update when another one starts before the first has *fully* finished server-side, even though the client-side "network is idle" signal was already satisfied. This is the single most subtle bug the catalog holds: the fix is moving a wait to the *start* of every field-changing helper, not just the end, and it was only caught because one particular field kept failing no matter how generous the *trailing* wait was made.
- **Data problems masquerading as code problems.** A field reports "no valid option" or a downstream calculation reports "undetermined" — not because a selector is wrong, but because the underlying test data doesn't actually satisfy the business rule the app is enforcing (e.g., a value from one category doesn't correspond to the specific record it's attached to). The catalog explicitly calls this out as its own category, because the instinct to "fix" it with more waits or retries only wastes attempts on something no amount of timing will ever resolve.

## Survey before you fix

The catalog's biggest structural change came from recognizing a costly pattern: discovering an entire never-before-adapted section of a form one failure at a time, via repeated full test re-runs, is slow — each run pays a real login and a full replay of everything before the failing step just to reveal the *next* single surprise. The fix was a front-loaded discovery workflow: write a disposable probe script that drives the whole flow once in a single session, filling fields with a best-guess pattern as it goes, and snapshotting the page's DOM after every meaningful step. Diffing consecutive snapshots reveals every field's true selector and widget type, and — just as importantly — which fields only appear after an intermediate save rather than reacting live to an earlier choice. Once the survey confirms the entire path end to end, the real fix gets ported into the actual test/page code in one pass, needing only a single full validation run afterward instead of many. The probe itself is always deleted before the work is considered done — it's a disposable instrument, not part of the delivered fix.

## Guardrails

- **A hard cap on diagnostic attempts per failure.** If a fixed number of distinct attempts on the same failure point don't resolve it, the skill stops instead of continuing to add speculative waits or retries hoping something sticks — a discipline specifically against the instinct to keep guessing under time pressure. It reports the exact failure point, what was tried and why each attempt didn't work, and a best-effort classification of what kind of unknown this is (an undocumented pattern, a genuine data problem, or an environment issue unrelated to the automation itself).
- **Atomic commits.** One unrelated bug per commit — except when an entire coherent rework (porting one whole form from the old widget pattern to the new one) is genuinely a single unit of work, in which case splitting it into artificial per-field commits would be the wrong kind of granularity.
- **Data fixes are called out separately from code fixes.** A correction to underlying test data is never bundled into a code commit, and always carries an explicit note that it needs to be pushed to the shared/live data source the tests draw from before anything ships — a local-only data fix that never gets synced quietly reintroduces the same failure for the next person.
- **No stray artifacts.** Every disposable diagnostic probe is deleted, and the working tree is checked clean of anything unrelated, before the work is reported done.

## Why this is a reusable pattern, not a one-off script

The specific bugs in the catalog are tied to one migration and will age out once it's complete. What's reusable is the shape of the skill itself: an accumulating catalog that turns repeated diagnosis into a lookup, a front-loaded survey step that trades one extra script for many fewer slow full-suite runs, and a hard stop-and-report guardrail instead of unbounded retry-and-hope. The same shape applies to any situation where a test suite has to track a moving target underneath it — a widget library swap, a design-system migration, an API version bump — not just this specific one.
